 separate this yellow  portion that I've drawn the these sugars  and the phosphate groups in from the  nitrogenous bases and the reason why is  because when we think about DNA and a  structure which we'll talk about more  depth in the next video is that it has  this the sugars and the phosphate kind  of make up what's called the backbone so  I want to label that here I'm going to  this side of the dotted line is going to  be the backbone specifically it's a  sugar phosphate backbone sugar phosphate  backbone and then the interior is this  portion here of course with a  nitrogenous bases we'll talk more about  that in detail in another video so  sometimes people will represent  single-stranded DNA like this a line to  represent I'd run it in the yellow to  indicate that that's the sugar phosphate  backbone and all of these little sort of  pink lines are present the nitrogenous  bases that are attached to the sugar  phosphate backbone so  I hope that video is helpful as far as  how to draw nucleic acids one last thing  I am a tutor if you live in Southern  California feel free to contact me at by  email at move University at gmail.com  for more information see the description  box below thank you for watching
 

